<BaseLayout
  title="Protein & Whey: Daily Targets & Research — Nawtch"
  description="ISSN-backed daily protein targets for active adults, what research says about timing vs. total intake, and where whey fits alongside whole foods."
  canonicalPath="/supplements/protein/"
  bodyClass="diets"
>
  <header>
    <div class="header-left">
      <h1>Protein / Whey</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">A convenient way to hit a daily protein target — the total matters far more than any single shake's timing.</p>
    </div>
    <a class="ghost-btn" href="/supplements/">← Back to Supplements</a>
  </header>

  <p class="field-hint" style="margin:-4px 0 14px;">This overlaps directly with macro tracking — our <a href="/calculators/macros/">Macro Calculator</a> can turn a calorie target into a specific daily protein gram target based on the ranges below.</p>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">How It Works</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        Whey is a fast-digesting, complete protein derived from milk, higher in the amino acid leucine than most
        other protein sources — leucine is a key trigger for muscle protein synthesis. Whey isn't nutritionally
        unique in what it does, though: it's a convenient, pre-portioned way to add protein to a day, functioning
        the same as any other complete protein source (meat, eggs, dairy, legumes) once it's digested and its
        amino acids enter circulation.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">The Research</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        The research here is less about whether protein matters — it clearly does — and more about how much you
        need and whether timing matters as much as it's often marketed.
      </p>
      <strong>Position stand:</strong> Jäger R, Kerksick CM, Campbell BI, et al. "International Society of Sports
      Nutrition Position Stand: Protein and Exercise," <em>Journal of the International Society of Sports
      Nutrition</em>, 2017. Recommends <strong>1.4–2.0g of protein per kg of body weight per day</strong> for
      most exercising individuals to support training adaptations, rising to <strong>2.3–3.1g/kg/day</strong> in
      resistance-trained individuals during a calorie deficit, specifically to help preserve lean mass while
      cutting.
      <br /><br />
      <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5477153/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the position stand →</a>
      <br /><br />
      <strong>Timing vs. total intake:</strong> Schoenfeld BJ, Aragon AA, Krieger JW. "The effect of protein
      timing on muscle strength and hypertrophy: a meta-analysis," <em>Journal of the International Society of
      Sports Nutrition</em>, 2013. Found that any apparent benefit of protein timed around workouts was almost
      entirely explained by the timed group also eating more total protein overall — not the timing itself. A
      companion review by the same authors suggests spreading protein across roughly 3–4 meals a day, at about
      20–40g per meal, as a practical distribution — but the headline finding is that total daily intake is the
      dominant factor, not hitting a narrow post-workout "window."
      <br /><br />
      <a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3879660/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the meta-analysis →</a>
      <br /><br />
      On whey specifically: the NIH Office of Dietary Supplements describes whey as one complete protein source
      among many, digesting faster and higher in leucine than casein — useful attributes, but not evidence that
      whey supplementation itself confers a benefit beyond what the same total protein intake would provide from
      other whole-food sources.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">Typical Dosing</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        Think in terms of total daily protein, not shake count: 1.4–2.0g/kg/day for most active people, up to
        2.3–3.1g/kg/day for resistance-trained individuals in a calorie deficit, per the ISSN position stand
        above. A typical whey scoop provides 20–30g of protein — useful for filling a gap in your day's total,
        not a required addition on top of an already-adequate diet.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">Who Should Be Cautious</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        Whey is derived from milk and unsuitable for anyone with a dairy allergy; those with lactose intolerance
        often tolerate whey isolate better than whey concentrate, which retains more lactose. Very high total
        protein intake sustained over time should be discussed with a doctor if you have existing kidney disease,
        since impaired kidneys have a reduced capacity to process protein waste products.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">Learn More</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <a href="https://ods.od.nih.gov/factsheets/ExerciseAndAthleticPerformance-HealthProfessional/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NIH Office of Dietary Supplements — Dietary Supplements for Exercise and Athletic Performance</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
